\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{An interacting positive-energy scalar Wightman field}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For #{m_0>0} and #{0<\lambda /m_0^2<\eta}, with #{\eta>0}
as in \ref{fdip-00IC}, the sequential state of
\ref{fdip-00I6} reconstructs a neutral scalar Wightman field in
#{1+1} dimensions. It consists of a positive-inner-product complex
Hilbert space #{\mathcal H}, a unit cyclic vacuum #{\Omega}, a
strongly continuous unitary representation #{U} of the proper
orthochronous Poincaré group, and an operator-valued tempered
distribution #{\varphi} on the common invariant dense domain
##{\mathcal D=\operatorname{span}\{
  \varphi(f_1)\cdots\varphi(f_n)\Omega:
  n\ge0,\ f_j\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^{1,1};\mathbb C)\}.}
Its smeared operators are linear in tests,
satisfy #{\varphi(f)^{\dagger}\supset\varphi(\overline f)} on
#{\mathcal D}, transform as a scalar under #{U}, and obey
##{[\varphi(f),\varphi(g)]\big|_{\mathcal D}=0}
whenever the supports are spacelike separated. The self-adjoint
translation generators #{(H,P)} have joint spectrum in
##{\{(E,p):E\ge|p|\}.}
The invariant vacuum space is #{\mathbb C\Omega}. The Euclidean
Schwinger forms are exactly the ultraviolet-then-volume moment
limits in \ref{fdip-00I6}. The Euclidean law is non-Gaussian and
the physical field is not generalized free.}

\proof{
\p{The functional satisfies every actual Axiom A-C by
\ref{fdip-00I8}, with the canonical covariance and entire
moment estimates of \ref{fdip-00I6}. Thus
\citet{Theorem 2.6, pp. 290-291}{frohlich1974verification} applies.
It is an abstract theorem about such a functional; its hypotheses
do not require the Gaussian displays in Section 3 of that paper.}

\p{The theorem supplies the Wightman theory and identifies its
Hilbert space, time-zero field and energy-momentum operators with
the functional reconstruction. Poincaré covariance, the forward-cone
spectral condition and locality are part of its Wightman conclusion.
The possible vacuum-uniqueness exception is removed by the mixing
of \ref{fdip-00IE} and Proposition D of the same source. The
non-Gaussian and non-generalized-free conclusions follow from
\ref{fdip-00II}, whose proof uses the identified state and
mixing rather than these conclusions.}
}
